{
  "dom_id": "step-q116853",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "If ${f}^{′}(x)=-|2x-5|$ and $f(1)=0,$ which of the following could be the graph of a continuous function  $y=f(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "The graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/5f48f6278983a74be84b95984808b23745ab4960dbd427f942efe18df86768e8.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\,$ $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2})$ | $2$ | $(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. Consider the vertical line  $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\n- Since we are told that $f(1)=0,$ we can draw that point on the graph.\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(1,\\frac{5}{2}),$ the point at $x=1$ must be higher than the point at $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/1ff02109b863615e5274b9ac41306644d8d42ce62671e0e76ecbda6655d249e0.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is decreasing $↘$ on $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{2})$ and on $(\\frac{5}{2},\\infty )$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes up to the left of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ and goes down to the right of $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\n- Furthermore, since ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing to the left of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)>0$, and, since it is decreasing to the right of $x=\\frac{5}{2}$, we must have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ So, $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave up to concave down.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/931e7763569df18dd0aea8cd1363f6d8414f1a0cc8deddc964ba11a9c74fe127.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/ad5016295fb3d33baac6af6e74dd3b2cbf563c74aef78bf27e5aed28222db3e7.png)",
